A||N||5||V6pack||Towing backwards w/dolley||||10||
Z||000000||V6pack||01-13-2000||06:07 PM||||<BR>I did a "search" not wanting to ask an old question, but didn't find a good yea or nay post.<P>I am looking to tow an '88 Formula about 125 miles. Automatic, so bassakwards is indicated. I only have a light truck.<P>My question is...Will I have problems with front facia dragging?  Truck is small and not too high in the rear.<P>What do you all think? Will it drag?<BR>Could be a deal breaker....small budget!<P>Thanks,<BR>V6pack...with only two left now.||||reg||10
Z||000001||william_thorniii||01-13-2000||07:03 PM||||6-pack,<P>The issue is clear cut for automatics.  The rear wheels must be on the dolly while towing.  Heat buildup from a long tow could otherwise severly damage your transmission.  The clearance should not be an issue.  Ed Parks has towed over 700 Fiero's with the rear end on the dolly.  I am sure that you will not have any problems providing that you follow general towing practices and use good common sense.  Here is my Fiero towing checklist.<P>1) Latch hood (front compartment lid)<P>2) Latch sun roof. <P>3) If hood and or sun roof will not latch properly, remove them to ensure that they do not remove themselves while towing (again ask Ed)<P>4) Lock the steering wheel<P>5) Tie off the steering wheel with the seat belt.  It is cheap insurance.<P>6) Put transmission in park<P>7) Put parking brake on firmly<P>8) Double and triple check the wheel straps, hitch and safety chains<P>9) Avoid very abrupt elevation changes... traversing curbs could cause a problem with the nose... use good common sense<P>10) Enjoy your new Fiero<P>If I have left anything out, I am sure that our list friends will be more than happy to point it out.<P>Happy Fiero-ing,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000002||V6pack||01-13-2000||07:18 PM||||William,<P>Thanks for all the good information.<BR>I had intended to tow backwards.  You also pointed out some of the other  wise precautions I had not thought to ask about.<P>I appreciate the feedback. I will put that in my "save" section.<P>Once again, thanks! My low budget is left intact!   Good news for the sig. other.....who worries about such things for me.<P>Regards,<BR>Dave||||reg||14
Z||000003||theogre||01-13-2000||07:34 PM||||If you think you have some too nasty stuff that would whack the front then you could take it off. It's time consuming but not hard.||||reg||1
Z||000004||V6pack||01-13-2000||08:00 PM||||Yeah Ogre, I'm planning to take the tool box and to watch out for those steep driveways.<P>I just have to remember, backing up over a bump may have a different result than going forward over one.<P>I had a Jag XJ-S last year that had about two inches below the air dam....lost it going into one of those old streets with the real high curbs and steeeeep driveways. Non-Fiero content, but same terrible ($) result!<P>Thanks,<BR>Dave||||reg||1
